course: everyday
lesson: 44
title: The beach and the sea
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The sea is never as still as a pool: waves knock you over, the tide comes in and out, and hidden currents move water much faster than you can swim.
explain: At patrolled beaches in Australia, New Zealand, the UK and Ireland, lifeguards put up red and yellow flags; you swim between them, because that is the area they watch; in the USA you swim near a lifeguard tower that is open.
explain: A rip current is a narrow, fast stream of water flowing away from the shore; it does not pull you under, it pulls you out to sea.
explain: If a rip carries you out, stay calm and float, raise an arm and call for help, or swim parallel to the shore until you are out of the rip, and then swim back in at an angle.
explain: A boy on a Queensland beach who fights straight back against a rip gets tired and frightened, while the boy who floats or swims sideways soon escapes it, because the rip is narrow.
explain: A boy in Cornwall played at the edge of the water when a big wave knocked him over and pulled him out; his dad, who was watching closely, got to him in time.
explain: A family in Australia went to a beach with no lifeguards and swam where the water looked calm; it was a rip, and the father had to be rescued by a surfer.
fact: You should always swim at a beach with lifeguards.
fact: Red and yellow flags mark the area that lifeguards watch, and you swim between them.
fact: Lifeguards move the flags during the day, because the safe place changes as the tide and currents change.
fact: A red flag on its own means it is too dangerous to swim.
fact: You never swim alone; a grown-up should be watching you all the time.
fact: A rip current is a narrow, fast flow of water moving away from the shore.
fact: A rip current pulls you out to sea; it does not pull you under the water.
fact: Rips often look like a calmer, darker gap where fewer waves break.
fact: If a rip takes you, you float, signal for help, or swim parallel to the shore, which means sideways, along the beach.
fact: Swimming straight back against a rip makes you tired, because the current is often faster than you.
fact: If you see someone in trouble, you call a lifeguard or the emergency number instead of swimming out yourself.
fact: Waves can knock you over even in shallow water, so you never turn your back on the sea for long.
fact: Inflatable toys and lilos can be blown out to sea by wind or carried by currents.
fact: Calm-looking dark water between breaking waves can be a rip current.
fact: Many people who drown at the beach swim where there are no lifeguards.
exercise: Where should you swim on a beach in Australia? | Between the red and yellow flags | Wherever the water looks calmest | Far from the lifeguards, where it is quiet | Next to the rocks, where the waves are smaller
exercise: Why should you swim between the red and yellow flags? | It is the area the lifeguards are watching | It is where the water is always warmest | It is where there are never any waves | It is the only part of the sea with no fish
exercise: What does a red flag on its own usually mean on a beach? | It is too dangerous to swim | Swim only between the red flags | The water is very cold today | The lifeguards are having a break
exercise: What is a rip current? | A narrow, fast flow of water moving away from the shore | A huge wave that crashes onto the beach | A whirlpool that spins round and pulls you down to the bottom of the sea | A warm stream of water along the beach
exercise: What does a rip current do to a swimmer? | It carries them out away from the shore | It sucks them down under the water | It throws them up onto the sand | It spins them round in circles
exercise: What can a rip current look like from the beach? | A calmer, darker gap where fewer waves break | The place with the biggest, whitest, noisiest waves | A patch of bright blue, very shallow water | A line of seaweed lying on the sand
exercise: A girl is caught in a rip. Which way should she swim? | Parallel to the shore, out of the rip | Straight back towards the beach against it | Straight out to sea, further from land | Down under the water, below the current
exercise: Why does swimming straight back against a rip often fail? | The current is often faster than a swimmer can swim | The water there is too salty for anyone to float in it | Rips change direction every few seconds | The waves block the way to the beach
exercise: What is the best thing to do if you are caught in a rip and are tired? | Float, raise one arm and call for help | Wave both arms and keep swimming hard | Dive under the water to escape | Take off your swimsuit to swim faster
exercise: Why should you never swim alone? | Nobody would be there to help or get help if you got into trouble | Swimming alone makes the water feel colder | Lifeguards are only allowed to watch groups of people, never one swimmer on their own | It is harder to float on your own
exercise: A boy sees his friend being carried out by a rip. What should he do? | Shout for a lifeguard or call for help, without swimming out | Swim out at once to pull his friend in, because every second counts | Throw stones to show where his friend is | Wait quietly until his friend comes back
exercise: Why is it unsafe to float far out on a blow-up lilo? | Wind and currents can carry it out to sea | Lilos sink as soon as they get wet | The sun melts the plastic quickly | Fish are attracted to the bright colours
exercise: Why should you not turn your back on the sea for long while playing in the waves? | A big wave can knock you over without warning | The sea gets a little deeper every single minute | Turning around makes you dizzy | The lifeguards cannot see your face
exercise: Which is the safest beach for a family swim? | A beach with lifeguards on duty and flags up | An empty beach with no one else around to get in your way | A beach with a sign saying no swimming | A rocky beach far from the car park
test: A girl in New Zealand finds the flags are far away and wants to swim right in front of her towel instead. What is the safest choice? | Walk to the flags and swim between them | Swim in front of her towel, but not far out | Swim where some surfers are | Swim where the water looks calm and dark
test: A boy notices a calm, dark strip of water between breaking waves and thinks it looks like the best place to swim. Why is he wrong? | That calm strip may be a rip current flowing out | Dark water is always very cold | Calm water has more jellyfish | Dark water always means there is a shark swimming close by
test: Which is true about rip currents? | They pull you away from the shore, not under the water | They always pull you down to the bottom | They only happen during storms | They happen only far out in the middle of the ocean, never near beaches
test: A boy caught in a rip feels it carrying him out. He is a good swimmer. What should he do? | Swim sideways, along the beach, until the pull stops | Swim with all his strength straight back to the sand | Dive to the bottom and walk back | Shout and kick until he is back on shore
test: A girl caught in a rip is too tired to swim. What will most likely keep her safe? | Floating on her back and raising an arm to signal | Trying hard to swim straight back against the current | Going under to look for the bottom | Taking off her life jacket so she can move faster
test: Why do lifeguards move the flags during the day? | The safe area changes as the tide and currents change | They are bored of the same place | To make people walk further along the sand to the café and shops | To let the sand dry under them
test: Two sisters on an Irish beach want to go in the sea while their dad sleeps on the sand. What should they do? | Wake him up so he can watch them swim | Go in quickly while he is asleep | Swim only in the shallow part | Take turns swimming while the other watches from the sand
test: A man sees a child struggling far out in the water. What is the safest thing to do? | Tell a lifeguard or call the emergency number at once | Swim out to the child straight away, without anything to float on | Run into the water to wave at the child | Wait and see if the child gets back alone
test: red flag at the beach, quick swim? | No, it's too dangerous to swim | Yes, just near the edge | Yes, if lifeguards are there | Yes, red means swim here
test: being pulled out from the beach!! | Float, signal, or swim parallel to the shore | Swim hard straight back to the sand, fast as you can | Dive under the water | Wave and swim straight in
test: that dark calm gap in the waves looks nice to swim | It may be a rip, stay away | Yes, calm water is safest | Yes, waves are dangerous | Swim there with a lilo
test: my mate's in trouble out there, swim out? | No, tell a lifeguard or call the emergency number | Yes, swim out fast | Yes, grab a lilo and paddle out to him as fast as you can | Wait to see if he waves
test: no lifeguards here but the water looks super calm | Go to a beach with lifeguards instead | Swim in the calm bit, it is safest when there are no waves | Swim out past the waves | Swim alone so you go faster
test: big waves at the rocks, gonna stand right at the edge for a pic | Stay well back, a wave can sweep you in | Go closer for a better pic, waves never reach that far | Stand with your back to the sea | Go if you hold your friend's hand
test: Mia says flat dark patches are the safe bit to swim. right? | No, they can be rip currents | Yes, flat water is always safe | Yes, dark water is deeper and safer | Yes, unless it is windy
test: my mate's cousin got sucked under by a rip and came up a mile out at sea, he swears | Exaggerated; a rip pulls you out, not under, so float, signal, or swim sideways | It is true, rips pull swimmers right down to the bottom and drag them along under the water | It proves you should never swim in the sea | It means you should swim hard straight back
test: heard the lifeguard put up the red flag but some guys swam anyway and one had to be rescued, my aunty saw it | Real lesson: a red flag means it is too dangerous to swim, so obey the flags | Real lesson: lifeguards put flags up for no reason, just to boss people about on the beach | It proves only adults ignore flags | It is gossip, so red flags do not matter
test: Jess got dumped by a wave and her swimsuit came half off, the boys are sharing it, should i | No, never share that; it is cruel and very private | Yes, because it is only a beach story | Yes, so she wears a different swimsuit | Yes, but only to the boys
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the effect of a rip current upon a swimmer? | It carries the swimmer away from the shore but does not pull him beneath the water | It pulls the swimmer directly to the sea bed and holds him there until the current weakens | It pushes the swimmer towards the shore | It holds the swimmer motionless in one place
exam: Identify the appearance commonly presented by a rip current. | A calmer and darker gap where fewer waves break | A line of high white breaking waves | A patch of bright green water lying close to the sand | A ring of bubbles around a swimmer
exam: It being given that a red flag is displayed alone upon a beach, determine its meaning. | That swimming is too dangerous | That the swimming area lies before it | That lifeguards are present all day | That surfing is permitted but not swimming
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance where another person is in difficulty at sea. | To summon a lifeguard or the emergency number, rather than to swim out oneself | To swim out at once to pull the person to shore, whatever the state of the sea | To wait upon the beach in silence | To throw sand towards the person to show the way
